"https://github.com/quarkusio/quarkus/blob/main/core/deployment/src/main/java/io/quarkus/deployment/builditem/ModuleEnableNativeAccessBuildItem.java[`io.quarkus.deployment.builditem.ModuleEnableNativeAccessBuildItem`, window=\"_blank\"]\n"
"[.description]\n"
"--\n"
"This will generate the equivalent of \"--enable-native-access moduleName\" for all runners of the generated application. This is being introduced currently to have the API in place, however it's currently very limited: when generating a runnable Jar we can only generate an Enable-Native-Access entry in the Manifest for the modules normally identified by ALL-UNNAMED. This is considered acceptable at the time of writing as extensions are generally placed on the classpath for both fast-jar and uber-jar packaging formats. We expect this to evolve as further packaging formats are introduced which would better leverage the module system. We specifically don't allow enabling native access for \"ALL-UNNAMED\" explicitly while using this API to encourage using the module names that a library has or will have in the near future: for this reason, when a module name is provided which doesn't exist, we map it to the unnamed module. It is not possible to allow native code access to other modules via an agent, other approaches will need to be identified to reconfigure at runtime.\n"
"--"
msgstr ""

msgid "https://github.com/quarkusio/quarkus/blob/main/core/deployment/src/main/java/io/quarkus/deployment/builditem/ModuleEnableNativeAccessBuildItem.java[`io.quarkus.deployment.builditem.ModuleEnableNativeAccessBuildItem`, window=\"_blank\"]"
msgstr ""

#, fuzzy
msgid "This will generate the equivalent of \"--enable-native-access moduleName\" for all runners of the generated application. This is being introduced currently to have the API in place, however it's currently very limited: when generating a runnable Jar we can only generate an Enable-Native-Access entry in the Manifest for the modules normally identified by ALL-UNNAMED. This is considered acceptable at the time of writing as extensions are generally placed on the classpath for both fast-jar and uber-jar packaging formats. We expect this to evolve as further packaging formats are introduced which would better leverage the module system. We specifically don't allow enabling native access for \"ALL-UNNAMED\" explicitly while using this API to encourage using the module names that a library has or will have in the near future: for this reason, when a module name is provided which doesn't exist, we map it to the unnamed module. It is not possible to allow native code access to other modules via an agent, other approaches will need to be identified to reconfigure at runtime."
msgstr "Isso irá gerar o equivalente a “--enable-native-access moduleName” para todos os executores do aplicativo gerado. Isso está sendo introduzido atualmente para disponibilizar a API, porém, no momento, ela é bastante limitada: ao gerar um JAR executável, só podemos gerar uma entrada “Enable-Native-Access” no manifesto para os módulos normalmente identificados por ALL-UNNAMED. Isso é considerado aceitável no momento da redação deste texto, uma vez que as extensões são geralmente colocadas no classpath tanto para o formato de empacotamento fast-jar quanto para o uber-jar. Esperamos que isso evolua à medida que novos formatos de empacotamento forem introduzidos, o que permitiria aproveitar melhor o sistema de módulos. Especificamente, não permitimos habilitar o acesso nativo para “ALL-UNNAMED” explicitamente ao usar esta API, a fim de incentivar o uso dos nomes de módulos que uma biblioteca possui ou terá em um futuro próximo: por esse motivo, quando é fornecido um nome de módulo que não existe, nós o mapeamos para o módulo sem nome. Não é possível permitir o acesso de código nativo a outros módulos por meio de um agente; será necessário identificar outras abordagens para reconfigurar em tempo de execução."

