msgid ""
"https://github.com/quarkusio/quarkus/blob/main/core/deployment/src/main/java/io/quarkus/deployment/builditem/ModuleOpenBuildItem.java[`io.quarkus.deployment.builditem.ModuleOpenBuildItem`, window=\"_blank\"]\n"
"[.description]\n"
"--\n"
"This will generate the equivalent of \"--add-opens=[openedModule/package(s)]=[openingModule]\" for all runners of the generated application. Some limitations apply which will depend on the launch mode of Quarkus; specifically, when generating a runnable Jar we can only open a module to ALL-UNNAMED; this is considered acceptable at the time of writing as extensions are generally placed on the classpath for both fast-jar and uber-jar packaging formats. We expect this to evolve as further packaging formats are introduced which would better leverage the module system. We specifically don't allow opening a module to \"ALL-UNNAMED\" explicitly to encourage using the module names that a library has or will have in the near future: for this reason, when a module name is provided which doesn't exist, we map it to the unnamed module. This should be treated as an implementation detail of the current packaging format and is most likely bound to evolve. When running integration tests, we aim to run the tests within module access rules consistent with the production packaging modes, but as we need to dynamically modify the module system's access restrictions we need to apply these rules to specific, targeted classloaders: we'll only instrument the unnamed module relating to the Quarkus app classloader. This should be effective but is based on the general understanding that libraries within Quarkus shouldn't use custom classloaders; should this assumption be violated, there's the risk of a \"module opens\" instruction to not be applied automatically, and users would be required to issue the matching command line flags.\n"
"--"
msgstr ""

msgid "https://github.com/quarkusio/quarkus/blob/main/core/deployment/src/main/java/io/quarkus/deployment/builditem/ModuleOpenBuildItem.java[`io.quarkus.deployment.builditem.ModuleOpenBuildItem`, window=\"_blank\"]"
msgstr ""

#, fuzzy
msgid "This will generate the equivalent of \"--add-opens=[openedModule/package(s)]=[openingModule]\" for all runners of the generated application. Some limitations apply which will depend on the launch mode of Quarkus; specifically, when generating a runnable Jar we can only open a module to ALL-UNNAMED; this is considered acceptable at the time of writing as extensions are generally placed on the classpath for both fast-jar and uber-jar packaging formats. We expect this to evolve as further packaging formats are introduced which would better leverage the module system. We specifically don't allow opening a module to \"ALL-UNNAMED\" explicitly to encourage using the module names that a library has or will have in the near future: for this reason, when a module name is provided which doesn't exist, we map it to the unnamed module. This should be treated as an implementation detail of the current packaging format and is most likely bound to evolve. When running integration tests, we aim to run the tests within module access rules consistent with the production packaging modes, but as we need to dynamically modify the module system's access restrictions we need to apply these rules to specific, targeted classloaders: we'll only instrument the unnamed module relating to the Quarkus app classloader. This should be effective but is based on the general understanding that libraries within Quarkus shouldn't use custom classloaders; should this assumption be violated, there's the risk of a \"module opens\" instruction to not be applied automatically, and users would be required to issue the matching command line flags."
msgstr "Isso gerará o equivalente a “--add-opens=[módulo(s) aberto(s)/pacote(s)]=[módulo de abertura]” para todos os executores do aplicativo gerado. Existem algumas limitações que dependem do modo de inicialização do Quarkus; especificamente, ao gerar um JAR executável, só é possível abrir um módulo para ALL-UNNAMED; isso é considerado aceitável no momento da redação deste texto, uma vez que as extensões são geralmente colocadas no classpath tanto para o formato de empacotamento fast-jar quanto para o uber-jar. Esperamos que isso evolua à medida que novos formatos de empacotamento forem introduzidos, o que permitiria aproveitar melhor o sistema de módulos. Especificamente, não permitimos a abertura de um módulo para “ALL-UNNAMED” de forma explícita, a fim de incentivar o uso dos nomes de módulo que uma biblioteca possui ou virá a possuir no futuro próximo: por esse motivo, quando é fornecido um nome de módulo que não existe, nós o mapeamos para o módulo sem nome. Isso deve ser tratado como um detalhe de implementação do formato de empacotamento atual e, muito provavelmente, está sujeito a evoluir. Ao executar testes de integração, nosso objetivo é executá-los dentro de regras de acesso a módulos consistentes com os modos de empacotamento de produção; no entanto, como precisamos modificar dinamicamente as restrições de acesso do sistema de módulos, precisamos aplicar essas regras a carregadores de classes específicos e direcionados: instrumentaremos apenas o módulo sem nome relacionado ao carregador de classes do aplicativo Quarkus. Isso deve ser eficaz, mas baseia-se no entendimento geral de que as bibliotecas no Quarkus não devem utilizar carregadores de classes personalizados; caso essa suposição seja violada, há o risco de que uma instrução “module opens” não seja aplicada automaticamente, e os usuários seriam obrigados a especificar os sinalizadores de linha de comando correspondentes."

